      "writtenOn": "2020-12-04T01:16:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Seems like we should notify the TCPM that VCONN has been disabled. The TCPM doesn\u0027t really expect this state though. If we just tell the TC state machine that VCONN is off, when the PE attempts to send an SOP\u0027, it will attempt a VCONN swap, but the partner will reject it because the partner isn\u0027t VCONN source.\n\nI guess as is, when the TCPC attempts to send an SOP\u0027 when VCONN is off (but the TC thinks it\u0027s on), the message will just timeout.\n\nAbe, what do you think?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 296,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 296,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "a343f1a468800561444eaa0b6fbeef417ba87418",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "664d4fe9_155a1d89",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 296,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-12-04T17:09:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If we\u0027re VCONN Source but not providing VCONN, and we attempt a VCONN Swap, the partner *may* Reject it. It could also Accept it, with the result that the partner would start sourcing VCONN. If we thought we were also doing an off-to-on VCONN Swap, I think we would treat the partner\u0027s PS_RDY as an Unexpected message and issue a Soft Reset. I can\u0027t immediately tell what the specified path back to normal is (or if there is one), but it\u0027s not a good situation to get into. Even if we never attempted a VCONN Swap, we\u0027d be confused about sourcing VCONN for the duration of the connection. Also, if this somehow happens after we\u0027ve entered a mode that depends on an active cable, that mode might stop working.\n\nI think we should probably treat this similarly to a VBUS OC event. I think it would take a Hard Reset to get us back on the same page with the partner, and I don\u0027t see how we would continue to act as a completely normal PD partner in the meantime.\n\nIf we have enough time before we overheat to attempt to swap away VCONN, we could try that, and the partner might be able to keep the cable up while we recover from this. That would have to override the policy decision about whether to be VCONN Source at the time, which might be more trouble than it\u0027s worth.",
      "parentUuid": "a9fc11e9_13bfa836",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 296,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 296,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "a343f1a468800561444eaa0b6fbeef417ba87418",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1f44dac8_0590232d",
        "filename": "driver/ppc/syv682x.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 296,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347775
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-12-04T18:04:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We can treat it similar to VBUS if you want, I followed the sn5s330\u0027s policy here. My opinion is it isn\u0027t needed though. If the device/cable is drawing over 3W on VCONN (max. allowed is 1.5W) for more than 100ms, then we should just give up. I think in this situation, SOP\u0027 timing out isn\u0027t a bad outcome; wouldn\u0027t that just result in it appearing that we have a passive cable?\n\nThe only situation we would see a VCONN OCP is a short circuit or a grossly non-compliant USB device. This isn\u0027t necessarily the case with VBUS, since the VBUS current limit is only 1.1X the nominal current, and the nature of VBUS-powered devices could be spiky. The VCONN limit is 2X, with the 100ms deglitch this is extremely permissive already.\n\nI think a fast VCS is probably out of scope for this change, the corner case that addresses is pretty specific.\n\nThe spec does touch on this by the way (robust source operation).",
      "parentUuid": "664d4fe9_155a1d89",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 296,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 296,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "a343f1a468800561444eaa0b6fbeef417ba87418",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
